One markdown document through both doors: `md:` importing the file, and
`md.document` parsing the same text as a string. Each is rendered in a div of
its own, and the markup inside the two is the same byte for byte --
`TestMarkdownDocumentMatchesScheme` in markdown_document_test.go compares them,
since a golden shows the two and a `deny` cannot say they are equal.

The document holds what each door used to answer differently: emphasis,
strong, inline code and a link, which md.document laid out as styled runs
rather than the scheme's span members; a nested list and a task list, which it
laid out as boxes and glyphs rather than `markup.list` and `markup.listItem`
(on html, `<ul>`/`<ol>`/`<li>` and a disabled checkbox); and two headings of one
text, whose anchors goldmark numbers.

deny out/none/html/index.html `[☐☑]` -- a task item is a checkbox on html, through either door
deny out/none/html/index.html `<span[^>]*style="font-(weight|style)` -- emphasis is <em>/<strong>, not a styled run

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import md "sngl:ui/markup/md"
import html "sngl:platform/html"
import doc "md:./guide.md"

output {
    none { html }
}

const guide = "# Guide\n\nSome *emphasis*, **strong** and `code`, with a [link](https://example.com).\n\n## Lists\n\n- one\n  - two\n    - three\n- [x] done\n- [ ] todo\n\n1. first\n2. second\n\n> A quotation.\n\n```go\nfunc main() \{\}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Lists\n\nThe second heading of one name.\n"

ui.window(title="doc") {
    ui.vbox {
        html.div(class="via-scheme") {
            doc.document()
        }
        html.div(class="via-document") {
            md.document(source=guide)
        }
    }
}
-- guide.md --
# Guide

Some *emphasis*, **strong** and `code`, with a [link](https://example.com).

## Lists

- one
  - two
    - three
- [x] done
- [ ] todo

1. first
2. second

> A quotation.

```go
func main() {}
```

---

## Lists

The second heading of one name.
